\subsection{Kitware}

The internship was carried out within the Software Solutions team at Kitware
Europe, located in Villeurbanne. Kitware specializes in the research and
development of open-source software and also provides custom software
development, training, consulting, and technical support services related to its
technologies. The company's areas of expertise include scientific and medical
visualization, computer vision, data analysis, and software process engineering.
Within this context, the Software Solutions team focuses primarily on scientific
and medical visualization, with a significant portion of its activities centered
around the trame framework, presented in \cref{sec:trame}.

\subsection{Scientific and Medical Visualization Tools}

This section introduces several frameworks commonly used in scientific and
medical visualization. An overview of their dependencies is provided in
\cref{fig:frameworks}.

\insertfigure {assets/images/frameworks.png} {width=\textwidth} {Frameworks
and module dependencies, purple: no code, green: C++, blue: JavaScript, red:
Python} {fig:frameworks}

Scientific and medical visualization applications address a broad range of
practical use cases. VTK (Visualization Toolkit) \cite{VTK}, developed by
Kitware, provides a robust foundation for building such applications.

For scientific visualization, \citev{ParaView}, also developed by Kitware, is an
open-source application built on top of VTK. It is widely used by researchers
and engineers to visualize and analyze large scientific datasets.  ParaView
offers extensive tools for data processing and rendering while scaling
efficiently to high-performance computing environments.

In the medical domain, 3D Slicer \cite{Slicer} is an open-source platform
dedicated to medical image visualization and analysis. It provides tools for
image segmentation, 3D reconstruction, registration, and quantitative analysis,
and is widely adopted in both research and clinical settings. Kitware also
contributes actively to its maintenance and development.

\subsection{Trame}

\label{sec:trame}

\subsubsection{Motivations}

Mastering VTK and 3D Slicer can be challenging due to their steep learning
curves, dependence on C++ expertise, and hardware requirements that may limit
their accessibility. Web-based solutions naturally address some of these
limitations by improving interoperability and accessibility. However, while most
researchers and engineers are familiar with Python, they often possess limited
experience in web development or C++. A study of researchers' software habits
\cite{researchers_habits} reports that Python is used by 67\% of researchers
(\cref{fig:researchers_habits_python}).

\insertfigure {assets/images/researchers-python} {width=\textwidth} {(source:
\cite{researchers_habits})} {fig:researchers_habits_python}

The same study also highlights the widespread adoption of open-science
practices, with open-source software being used more frequently than commercial
alternatives, as illustrated in \cref{fig:researchers_habits_open_source}.

\insertfigure {assets/images/researchers-open-source} {height=0.3\textheight}
{from \cite{researchers_habits}} {fig:researchers_habits_open_source}

Trame \cite{trame} is an open-source Python framework that enables the rapid
development of custom applications without requiring expertise in compilation,
C++, or web technologies. Consequently, it aligns closely with the needs of
researchers and engineers. While VTK.js (a JavaScript implementation of VTK)
allows VTK-based applications to run in a web environment, it still requires
knowledge of JavaScript. Similarly, although ParaView can be used without C++
programming skills, it does not generate web applications that can be deployed
and accessed from arbitrary devices.

Trame's user interface is built from \citev{VueJS} components. Vue is a
framework for developing interactive web interfaces through modular and reusable
components and generally offers a lower barrier to entry than alternatives such
as React or Angular. Adding a new UI element to Trame typically involves
implementing a Vue component and wrapping it as a Trame widget, after which it
can be seamlessly integrated into an application. Vuetify \cite{Vuetify}
provides a comprehensive collection of Vue components covering common interface
elements such as checkboxes, sliders, and tables. The
\definerepo{https://github.com/Kitware/trame-vuetify}{trame-vuetify} package
exposes these components within Trame, enabling the rapid creation of polished
user interfaces. \Cref{fig:trame-easy-ui} illustrates how reactive applications
can be developed with only a small amount of code.

\insertfigure{assets/images/trame-easy-ui}{height=0.3\textheight}{Trame code
snippet (source: \href{https://kitware.github.io/trame/guide/}{trame guide},
upscaled with \citev{ChatGPT})}{fig:trame-easy-ui}

Trame is organized around widgets, each providing either specialized Vue.js
components or functionality based on interactions between JavaScript and Python.
One of the framework's main strengths is its ecosystem of domain-specific
widgets. For scientific visualization, \refrepo{trame-vtk} enables the
integration of VTK into Trame applications, while
\definerepo{https://github.com/KitwareMedical/trame-slicer}{trame-slicer}
provides access to 3D Slicer functionality.

Both \refrepo{trame-vtk} and \refrepo{trame-slicer} support remote rendering:
the application executes and renders on the Python side, streaming the result as
an HTML image while user interactions are transmitted back to the server.  This
mechanism is implemented through
\definerepo{https://github.com/Kitware/trame-rca}{trame-rca} (Remote Controlled
Area), on which both widgets depend.

\Cref{fig:trame-application} illustrates the type of advanced applications that
can be developed using \refrepo{trame-vtk} and \refrepo{trame-vuetify}.

\insertfigure{assets/images/trame-application}{height=0.3\textheight}{Example of
a Trame application from the Trame tutorial}{fig:trame-application}

Kitware sought to develop custom medical visualization applications. Although
\citev{VolView} already existed, it operated entirely within the user's browser.
To address this limitation, trame-slicer was developed as a Trame widget that
leverages the capabilities of 3D Slicer \cite{Slicer} through its Python
bindings. A complete web application showcasing the capabilities of
trame-slicer, known as the Medical Viewer, was subsequently created. It provides
functionality comparable to that of 3D Slicer, including navigation through
different views of volumetric data (which may originate from \citev{DICOM}),
segmentation tools, and annotation features. \Cref{fig:medical-viewer-ui} shows
its interface.

\subsection{Features}

\subsubsection{Client/Server Shared State}

Trame can be viewed as a Python abstraction layer built on top of \citev{VueJS}.
In VueJS, variables can be accessed from both the \texttt{template}
(client-side) and \texttt{script} (server-side) sections through references
(\texttt{ref}). Similarly, Trame enables variables to be shared between the
Python and web components of an application.

These variables are synchronized between the server and client through a
\texttt{state} object using WebSocket communication. User interface elements and
their associated JavaScript code can invoke Python functions or modify shared
state variables, which remain synchronized across both environments. This
architecture is illustrated in \cref{fig:trame-slide}.

\insertfigure{assets/images/trame-slide}{height=0.2\textheight}{Trame
Model--View--ViewModel principle (source:
\href{https://kitware.github.io/trame/guide/}{trame guide})}{fig:trame-slide}

\subsubsection{Typed States}

\label{sec:typedstates}

Binding shared variables to a singleton state object (a class with a single
instance) can become difficult to manage in large code bases. Typed states
address this issue by providing typed references to variables stored within the
state object. A typed state can be created anywhere in the code, automatically
associating a local variable with a corresponding entry in the shared state.
This mechanism is particularly useful when binding shared variables to user
interface components, as discussed later in this report.
